me=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003ek\u003csub\u003e5\u003c/sub\u003e\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e24.59\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e45.03\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e19.60\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e18.08\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e31.41\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c7\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e32.30\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eR\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e18.59\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e25.85\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e34.20\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e19.43\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e12.62\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c7\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e10.96\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003c/tbody\u003e \u003c/colgroup\u003e \u003c/table\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003c/p\u003e \u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv id=\"Sec10\" class=\"Section2\"\u003e \u003ch2\u003e3.2 Effect of glucose on GLPF\u003c/h2\u003e \u003cp\u003eThe best combination was screened out by orthogonal experiment and the experiment was verified. Glucose was used as modifier to prepare GLPF, which was added at the beginning of the condensation and 3 h, 2 h, 1 h, 30 min, 15 min, and 5 min before the end of the condensation, to explore the effect of the addition stage of the reaction on GLPF, the structure and physical properties of GLPF were determined in Table\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan refid=\"Tab3\" class=\"InternalRef\"\u003e3\u003c/span\u003e.\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003e \u003cdiv class=\"gridtable\"\u003e\u003ctable float=\"Yes\" id=\"Tab3\" border=\"1\"\u003e \u003ccaption language=\"En\"\u003e \u003cdiv class=\"CaptionNumber\"\u003eTable 3\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\"CaptionContent\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eEffects of glucose addition stages on different types of resins\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/div\u003e \u003c/caption\u003e \u003ccolgroup cols=\"6\"\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c1\" colnum=\"1\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c2\" colnum=\"2\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c3\" colnum=\"3\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c4\" colnum=\"4\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c5\" colnum=\"5\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c6\" colnum=\"6\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cthead\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/th\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eReagent\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eAddition stage\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003epH\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eSolid content(%)\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eFormaldehyde Emission(mg∙L\u003csup\u003e\u0026minus;\u0026thinsp;1\u003c/sup\u003e)\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003c/thead\u003e \u003ctbody\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003ePF\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eReference resin without modification reagent\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e13.87\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e42.70\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.069\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eLPF\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eLignin\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eWith the addition of 15 wt% lignin\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e11.85\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e45.20\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.093\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\" morerows=\"6\" rowspan=\"7\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eGLPF\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eGlucose\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eWith starting reagent\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e-*\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e-\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e-\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eGlucose\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e3 h before the end\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003eof condensation\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e-\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e-\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e-\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eGlucose\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e2 h before the end\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003eof condensation\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e-\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e-\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e-\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eGlucose\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1 h before the end\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003eof condensation\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e11.25\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e48.20\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.097\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eGlucose\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e30 min before the end of condensation\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e11.26\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e49.33\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.094\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eGlucose\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e15 min before the end of condensation\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e11.32\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e48.60\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.12\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eGlucose\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e5 min before the end\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003eof condensation\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e11.39\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e49.50\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.092\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colspan=\"3\" nameend=\"c3\" namest=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eChinese national standard\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e\u0026gt;\u0026thinsp;7\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e\u0026gt;\u0026thinsp;35%\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c6\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e\u0026lt;\u0026thinsp;0.5%\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003c/tbody\u003e \u003c/colgroup\u003e \u003ctfoot\u003e \u003ctr\u003e\u003ctd colspan=\"6\"\u003e*: The product was agglomerated, and the properties of the resin cannot be determined according to the Chinese national standard met.\u003c/td\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e \u003c/tfoot\u003e \u003c/table\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003eTable\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan refid=\"Tab3\" class=\"InternalRef\"\u003e3\u003c/span\u003e showed the effect of glucose addition stage on the pH, solid content, and formaldehyde emission of different types of resin. Either lignin or glucose will lower the pH of the resin, and the resin had a higher solid content at the same time. The formaldehyde emission in the system also increased slightly after adding lignin and glucose but far lower than the Chinese national standard (E\u003csub\u003e0\u003c/sub\u003e). At the beginning of the condensation and 3 h, 2 h before the end of the condensation, the resin would agglomerate, which may be due to that molecular chain of glucose will be partially broken and the cross-linking degree between glucose, phenol, and formaldehyde could be increased at hyperthermic water bath under strong alkali surroundings.\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003eFigure\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan refid=\"Fig1\" class=\"InternalRef\"\u003e1\u003c/span\u003e displayed the bonding strength and free phenol of different resin samples. The bonding strength and free phenol of PF, LPF, and GLPF were in compliance with the Chinese national standard. It can be noted from Fig.\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan refid=\"Fig1\" class=\"InternalRef\"\u003e1\u003c/span\u003e, as the substitution amount of lignin to formaldehyde was 15 wt%, the bonding strength increased from 0.75 MPa to 0.96 MPa, the addition of glucose increased the bonding strength to 1.19 MPa. After adding lignin, the free phenol in the system was lower than the Chinese national standard, although the content of free phenol increased to 5.60% in comparision to PF, 0.39%. Fortunately, after the addition of glucose, the content of free phenol in the system decreased gradually, when glucose was added at the beginning of condensation, the content of free phenol in the system decreased from 5.60\u0026ndash;3.32%, which decreased by 41%. When the glucose was added 5 min before the end of condensation, the lowest content of free phenol, 1.17%, was achieved, which decreased by 79% in comparison to LPF.\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003e  \u003c/p\u003e \u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv id=\"Sec11\" class=\"Section2\"\u003e \u003ch2\u003e3.3 FT-IR analysis\u003c/h2\u003e \u003cp\u003eIt can be seen from the FT-IR (Fig.\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan refid=\"Fig2\" class=\"InternalRef\"\u003e2\u003c/span\u003e), the main characteristic absorption peaks of LPF and GLPF were similar to PF: the absorption band around at 3365 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e, 1362 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e, and 1020 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e was attributed to the aromatic and aliphatic -OH, the absorption band at 2844 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e was related to the asymmetric vibration of -CH\u003csub\u003e2\u003c/sub\u003e-, and the absorption band at 1600 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e, 1481 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e was benzene ring skeleton, which indicated that the addition of lignin or glucose did not affect the main structure of the PF after substitution or modification. A semiquantitative analysis method was adopted to analyze the structural changes of PF, LPF, and GLPF (Table\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan refid=\"Tab4\" class=\"InternalRef\"\u003e4\u003c/span\u003e) ( Huerta et al. 2021; Zhao et al. 2020; Xie et al. 2018). The aromatic skeleton vibration occurred at 1600 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e, which was used for normalization which intensity was set to 1.000, since it was nearly unchanged in the three systems (Li et al. 2016). The total amount of aromatic and aliphatic-OHs increased in LPF and GLPF, among which phenolic-OHs can activate the free ring sites in lignin, making them more reactive (Zhang et al. 2013; Doherty et al. 2011; Jin et al. \u003cspan citationid=\"CR25\" class=\"CitationRef\"\u003e2010\u003c/span\u003e). The band at 3365 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e of LPF decreased in the hydroxyl content of the phenol, which may be due to the fact that participation of lignin in the reaction would result in the scission of the macromolecular chain, the reduction in the content of phenolic-OHs, and the decrease in the reactivity. At the same time, the decreased phenolic-OHs may result in an increase in the content of free phenol in the system, which was conformed to the results in Fig.\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan refid=\"Fig1\" class=\"InternalRef\"\u003e1\u003c/span\u003e, compared with PF, the total content of aromatic and aliphatic-OHs in GLPF was significantly increased, which may be due to the fact that glucose contains more aliphatic-OHs. The bonding strength was enhanced compared with PF and LPF could possibly because the characteristic peaks of hydroxyl at 1020 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e, 1362 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e, 3365 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e and the alkyl group at 1481 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e, 2844 cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e in GLPF were higher than those in PF and LPF, which lead to the adsorption or complex reaction with the polar functional groups such as the hydroxyl groups, the alkyl groups, and the hemiacetal groups on the wood surface more readily.\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003e  \u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003e \u003cdiv class=\"gridtable\"\u003e\u003ctable float=\"Yes\" id=\"Tab4\" border=\"1\"\u003e \u003ccaption language=\"En\"\u003e \u003cdiv class=\"CaptionNumber\"\u003eTable 4\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv class=\"CaptionContent\"\u003e \u003cp\u003ePeaks and remarks of FT-IR spectra of PF, LPF, and GLPF\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/div\u003e \u003c/caption\u003e \u003ccolgroup cols=\"5\"\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c1\" colnum=\"1\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c2\" colnum=\"2\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c3\" colnum=\"3\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c4\" colnum=\"4\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv align=\"left\" class=\"colspec\" colname=\"c5\" colnum=\"5\"\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003cthead\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\" morerows=\"1\" rowspan=\"2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eWave number\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003e(cm\u003csup\u003e-1\u003c/sup\u003e)\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colspan=\"3\" nameend=\"c4\" namest=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eAbsorbance relative value\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\" morerows=\"1\" rowspan=\"2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eAssignments\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003ePF\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eLPF\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003cth align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eGLPF\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/th\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003c/thead\u003e \u003ctbody\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1020\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.677\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.814\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e3.415\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eν(-OH)\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1362\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.816\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.956\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e2.613\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eν(-OH)\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1481\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.886\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.751\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.853\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eδ am(C-H)\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1600\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.000\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.000\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.000\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eν(C\u0026thinsp;=\u0026thinsp;C)\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e2844\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.465\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.363\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e3.571\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eν(-CH\u003csub\u003e2\u003c/sub\u003e-)\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003ctr\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c1\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e3365\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c2\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.921\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c3\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e0.695\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c4\"\u003e \u003cp\u003e1.095\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003ctd align=\"left\" colname=\"c5\"\u003e \u003cp\u003eν(-OH)\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/td\u003e \u003c/tr\u003e \u003c/tbody\u003e \u003c/colgroup\u003e \u003c/table\u003e\u003c/div\u003e \u003c/p\u003e \u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv id=\"Sec12\" class=\"Section2\"\u003e \u003ch2\u003e3.4 Analysis of curing behavior\u003c/h2\u003e \u003cp\u003eThe thermal decomposition and thermal stability of PF, LPF, and GLPF were shown in Fig.\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan refid=\"Fig3\" class=\"InternalRef\"\u003e3\u003c/span\u003e. The thermal degradation of the resin was mainly divide into three stages: postcuring, thermal reforming, and ring stripping (Zhang et al. 2013). The first stage thermal decomposition occurred at 100\u0026ndash;350\u0026deg;C, the heat loss was mainly due to the overflow of small molecular organics during the cross-linking process of the end groups and the escape of water during the condensation reaction of methylol groups (Khan et al. 2004; Mohamad Ibrahim et al. 2011). The second stage thermal decomposition occurred at 350\u0026ndash;600\u0026deg;C, the heat loss was caused by the water generated by the breakdown of methylene linkages and reaction of methylene groups with phenolic hydroxyl groups (Wang et al. 2009; Tejado et al. 2007; Boeriu et al. 2004; Trick et al. 1995). The third stage thermal decomposition occurred at 600\u0026ndash;800\u0026deg;C, the heat loss was mainly due to the formation of carbon monoxide and methane induced by the degradation of methylene bridges (Jackson and Conley \u003cspan citationid=\"CR24\" class=\"CitationRef\"\u003e1964\u003c/span\u003e; Qiao et al. 2015). When the temperature reached 800\u0026deg;C, the residual carbon content of PF, LPF, and GLPF were 59%, 58%, and 51%, respectively. The addition of lignin and glucose will decrease the thermal stability of the resin, which was due to the lower activity of lignin and glucose in comparison to formaldehyde, thus low crosslinking density of GLPF was prepared by incomplete reaction. Some researchers also found that the thermal stability of phenolic resin mixed with lignin is lower than PF(Qiao et al. 2015; Alma and Kelley \u003cspan citationid=\"CR2\" class=\"CitationRef\"\u003e2000\u003c/span\u003e; Chen et al. 2019).\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003e  \u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003eThe exothermic peak of DSC curves was due to the polycondensation of methylol and phenol to form a methylene bond(V\u0026aacute;zquez et al. 2005). As shown in Fig.\u0026nbsp;\u003cspan refid=\"Fig4\" class=\"InternalRef\"\u003e4\u003c/span\u003e, the DSC curves of PF, LPF and GLPF were similar. The curing temperature of PF, LPF were 107℃ and 106℃, respectively, which indicated that the addition of lignin had no obvious effect on the curing temperature of PF, LPF. After modification by adding glucose, the curing temperature of GLPF was 93\u0026deg;C, which was significantly lower than PF and LPF. The presence of at least one distinctive exothermic peak in DSC diagrams was a characteristic of curing or crosslinking of resol phenolic adhesives, as a result of condensation polymerization reactions in the adhesives (Chai et al. 2016; Christiansen and Gollob \u003cspan citationid=\"CR10\" class=\"CitationRef\"\u003e1985\u003c/span\u003e). An interesting phenomenon was that PF showed a second exothermic peak at 146℃, compared with LPF and GLPF, which might because higher pH had a great influence on the DSC curve and the DSC curves showed two peaks(He and Yan \u003cspan citationid=\"CR20\" class=\"CitationRef\"\u003e2004\u003c/span\u003e, \u003cspan citationid=\"CR21\" class=\"CitationRef\"\u003e2005\u003c/span\u003e). In the process of preparing plywood, GLPF had a lower curing temperature, which not only saved the heating time during the curing process, but also reduced the manufacturing cost and protect resources at a lower temperature. The lower curing temperature had a good prospect and economic significance for the industrial mass production of wood-based panel and application widely.\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003e  \u003c/p\u003e \u003c/div\u003e \u003cdiv id=\"Sec13\" class=\"Section2\"\u003e \u003ch2\u003e3.5 Mechanism analysis\u003c/h2\u003e \u003cp\u003eThe structure of lignin was generally considered to be a macromolecule of phenylpropane (C9) units linked by an ether bond and carbon-carbon bond, which contained hydroxyl groups and aldehyde groups(Gao et al. 2021; Cheng et al.2017; Wang et al. 2017; Vanholme et al. 2010; Huang et al. 2022 Wang et al. 2018). The hydroxyl units in the lignin structure reacted with aldehyde, the aldehyde structural units reacted with phenol, which increased the crosslink density and increased the bonding strength of the resin. Furthermore, lignin underwent a certain degree of self-polymerization occurred at high temperatures(Gregorich et al. 2021), which may also increase the bonding strength.\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003eThe aldehyde groups structure in glucose may be related to the resinification reaction, which occurred between the aldehyde groups of glucose with the phenol ring(Sui et al. 2019), moreover, the hydroxyl polar groups in glucose can adsorb or react with polar functional groups such as the hydroxyl groups, the alkyl groups, and the hemiacetal groups on the wood surface to further improve the bonding strength of the resin.\u003c/p\u003e \u003cp\u003eWhen glucose was heated under alkaline condition, the molecular chain was easy to break down and more active groups were exposed to react with phenol and formaldehyde in the early reaction. Therefore, the reaction between phenol and formaldehyde was insufficient, resulting the free phenol was not significantly reduced. When glucose was added close to the end of the condensation, the reaction between phenol and formaldehyde was nearly complete, some free phenol and free aldehyde groups in the system could continue to react with the active hydroxyl groups and aldehyde groups in glucose, so that the content of free phenol was significantly reduced.\u003c/p\u003e \u003c/div\u003e"},{"header":"4 Conclusions","content":"\u003cp\u003eLignin was directly used to substitute formaldehyde to prepare the low-toxic and environmentally friendly LPF when F/P molar ratio was 1:2.0, the substitution amount of lignin to formaldehyde was 15 wt%, the amount of NaOH was 15 wt%, temperature was 95\u0026deg;C, time was 4 h, the amount of urea was 5 wt%. The addition of glucose further improved the properties of the resin. Compared with PF, the bonding strength of LPF was increased from 0.75 MPa to 0.96 MPa. After modification with glucose 5 min before the end of the condensation, the bonding strength further increased from 0.96 MPa to 1.19 MPa, which increased by 19%; and the free phenol was significantly decreased from 5.60% to 1.17%, which decreased by 79%. DSC results showed that the curing temperature of the GLPF decreased after modified by glucose, although the substitution of formaldehyde by lignin had no significant effect on the curing temperature and thermal stability of LPF in comparison to PF. The novel method of preparing environmentally friendly and low-toxic phenolic resin through substitution of formaldehyde by lignin and modification by glucose provides a way for the high value utilization of lignin and glucose.\u003c/p\u003e"},{"header":"Declarations","content":"\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eFounding\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThis work was supported by National Natural Science Foundation of China\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e(Grant No. 21464011) and the Scientific Research Program of Shihezi University\u0026nbsp;\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e(RCZK2021B09).\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eDeclaration of interest statement\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe authors declare no competing financial interest.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eData availability statement\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eThe raw and processed data required to reproduce these findings are available from the corresponding author upon request.\u003c/p\u003e\n"},{"header":"References","content":"\u003col\u003e\n    \u003cli\u003eAi XB, Feng SH, Shui T (2021) Effects of Alcell Lignin Methylolation and Lignin Adding Stage on Lignin-Based Phenolic Adhesives. Molecules 26: 6762. https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules26226762\u003c/li\u003e\n    \u003cli\u003eAlma MH, Kelley SS (2000) Thermal stability of novolak-type thermosettings made by the condensation of bark and phenol. Polym Degrad Stabil\u003cstrong\u003e\u0026nbsp;\u003c/strong\u003e68: 413-418. https://doi.org/10.1016/s0141-3910(00)00029-x\u003c/li\u003e\n    \u003cli\u003eAng AF, Ashaari Z, Lee SH, Md Tahir P, Halis R (2019) Lignin-based copolymer adhesives for composite wood panels-a review. Int J Adhes Adhes 95: 102408. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijadhadh.2019.102408\u003c/li\u003e\n    \u003cli\u003eArefmanesh M, Nikafshar S, Master ER, Nejad M (2022) From acetone fractionation to lignin-based phenolic and polyurethane resins. Ind Crop Prod 178: 114604. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.indcrop.2022.114604.\u003c/li\u003e\n    \u003cli\u003eBoeriu CG, Bravo D, Gosselink RJA, Van Dam JEA (2004) Characterisation of structure-dependent functional properties of lignin with infrared spectroscopy. Ind Crop Prod 20: 205-218. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.indcrop.2004.04.022\u003c/li\u003e\n    \u003cli\u003eChai YB, Liu JL, Zhao Y, Yan N (2016) Characterization of modified phenol formaldehyde resole resins synthesized in situ with various boron compounds. Ind Eng Chem Res 55: 9840-9850. https://doi.org/10.1021/acs.iecr.6b02156\u003c/li\u003e\n    \u003cli\u003eChen Y, Gong XW, Yang, GS, Li Q, Zhou N (2019) Preparation and characterization of a nanolignin phenol formaldehyde resin by replacing phenol partially with lignin nanoparticles. RSC Adv 9: 29255-29262. https://doi.org/10.1039/c9ra04827h\u003c/li\u003e\n    \u003cli\u003eCheng CB, Wang JZ, Shen DK, Xue JT, Guan S, Gu SP, Luo K (2017) Catalytic oxidation of lignin in solvent systems for production of renewable chemicals: A review. Polymer 9: 240. https://doi.org/10.3390/polym9060240\u003c/li\u003e\n    \u003cli\u003eCheng YY, Sui GH (2022) Synthesis and regulation mechanism of bio-oil-glucose phenolic resin using furfural as cross